hi there this is steve kaufman  um i want to talk today about the uh  earthquake in japan the tsunami the loss  of life the situation there  because quite honestly i just can't stop  thinking about it  um  i  lived in japan for nine years  first arrived there in uh january of  1971.  uh i have had dealings with japan for 40  years  my youngest son was born there  i lived there with my family  i've worked there  and i it's just been a big part of my  life i have  i have the greatest amount of respect  for  uh  japanese culture japan  and it i just can't imagine what it is  like  to be there  especially up in the northeast  where we're now hearing of ten thousand  possible  uh people who will have lost their lives  um  there's always unsettling  uh  development around their nuclear  facilities up there  there's talk about another aftershock of  a magnitude of seven  there's a shortage of of goods in stores  even in tokyo  obviously people are in a state of shock  there are millions of people without  heat and electricity  in the northeast which is a cold part of  japan  i cannot imagine  what it is like  and i visited kobe after the  earthquake in 1996  and it just seems like japan gets  slammed hit with these natural disasters  with with uh appalling  regularity  so all i wanted to say was that i know  as a canadian who has lived in japan who  has worked with japanese people in the  lumber business primarily and i know  many canadians who have worked with  japan  that  just about everyone has the  fondest possible feelings for  japanese people the greatest respect for  japanese people a great sense of  of  gratitude to have had the opportunity to  work with japanese people  over these many years  and you know i was thinking about the  qualities of the japanese that i find so  admirable and i came up with this this  one word that keeps on  coming back to me which is respect  r-e-s-p-e-c-t  and if you break it down  using the first letters of a number of  qualities that all come around this  whole respect word  japanese people have respect for each  other more than most countries where i  have lived or visited or worked everyone  is respected even uh someone who is a  has a relatively menial job is respected  people operate in an atmosphere of  respect and trust and we're seeing this  i think in the way people are responding  to the uh to the uh disaster  uh up uh in in the northeast  uh the second thing about japan is that  it's a relatively equal country it's  probably the most egalitarian country  in the world  there's no tremendous differences  between rich and poor people don't  flaunt their wealth  um  so it goes hand in hand with this  respect it's respect even though people  talk about japan as this vertical  society where you know the  the underlings have to bow  to the bosses and so forth in fact it's  not quite like that and it's it's very  much of an egalitarian society where  people where people respect each other  s so we got r-e-s s is for self-reliance  the japanese are not the kind of people  who expect someone else to do things for  them they don't have this  developed sense of entitlement that i  think we have here in north america they  know that they have to do things for  themselves and they're willing to do it  they're willing to buckle down and do  what they have to do which again we're  seeing in the reaction of ordinary  people  to to the the circumstances up in the  northeast  resp perseverance the japanese they stay  with it they don't they don't promise  you oh yeah no problem i can do that  after this and then not deliver they  deliver they stay with it until the job  is done  and and i think this is why they've been  successful in so many things they  persevere they stay with it and they're  not easily discouraged by adversity as  we're going to see as japan rebuilds  from this latest  tragedy  the next thing about the japanese  another e here r-e-s-p-e energy they're  very dynamic energetic people they're  energetic in their work and they're  energetic in play and especially this  younger generation of japanese who are  often the butt of criticism if i compare  them to when i was in japan in the 70s  they love to have a good time they're  great people to be with  full of energy  always looking for some new venture  something they can get into so they're  energetic people and they're going to  need that energy to rebuild  respect  r-e-s-p-e-c curiosity the japanese are  curious they're not afraid to learn they  go on trips overseas to study you'll  find japanese people studying flamenco  music in spain  or  you know buddhist  uh  you know philosophy in thailand or or  whatever or learning about new  technology or learning about their own  history  they're curious about the history of the  world they're they're very curious  they're interested in learning about  things  curious and finally the japanese are  thorough  they are thorough they are  people who pursue perfection now  pursuing perfection you're not likely to  achieve perfection but it's a large part  of why they are so creative and they are  a very creative people they have a very  unique culture and in recent years the  number of patents coming out of japan is  tremendous their influence on  on modern pop culture on  architecture on food on so many aspects  of our culture is is very very  significant so they are unique and  creative  uh culture and a lot of that culture  comes in my opinion comes from this  commitment to thoroughness they are not  a superficial people so  i am convinced that for all of these  reasons  the reasons that i respect in the  japanese that they will overcome all  these difficulties  and uh i'm sure they'll be criticizing  criticism flying around the government  should have done this tokyo electric  power company should have done that but  relative to what would have been the  situation in just about any other  country i can think of i think the  japanese will recover more quickly  and will start once again to build and  create  uh a dynamic society and culture and  economy so i really uh want to offer my  deepest sympathies uh to people in japan  and you know my  sense of you know gokuro  and i'm looking forward to my next trip  to japan thank you for listening
 

